Operating profile: provided by sutra (plugin or local `@import`). This file
holds project-specific guardrails only.

## Build and test

Use the task runner. It bakes in the right environment, flags, and
preconditions -- raw commands skip those and drift from what CI runs.

- `<runner> check` -- fast feedback loop (lint + unit tests). Keep it quick.
- `<runner> test` -- full test suite.
- `<runner> lint` -- linters and formatters.
- `<runner> build` -- produce the build artifact.
- `<runner> --list` -- full catalog of tasks.

<!-- NOTE: Replace `<runner>` with your actual entry point (for example a
Taskfile target, `make`, `just`, or an `npm`/`pnpm` script). Prefer the
runner over raw `<tool> <args>`; only reach past it for targeted debugging,
then re-run the task before you commit. -->

## Working agreement / invariants (example -- replace with your project's)

Example invariants a newcomer would not guess. Swap these for the rules
that actually bind changes in your repo:

- **Never weaken a test to make it pass.** If a test goes red, find the
  real cause. Do not loosen an assertion, add a blanket skip, or delete a
  case to get to green.
- **Keep each branch/PR focused on one logical change** so it reviews
  cleanly. Some teams squash to a single commit; others keep a reviewable
  commit sequence -- follow your project's merge convention.
- **Do not commit planning or agent-only files.** Scratch notes, task
  breakdowns, and session logs stay local (see `.gitignore`). Never stage
  them, even under a "commit everything" instruction.

<!-- Some teams require ASCII-only files (sutra's strict profile asks for it);
if yours does, state it here and verify with `file <path>`. -->

<!-- NOTE: Add project-specific invariants a newcomer could not guess -- a
required column order, a cache byte layout to preserve, a load-order
dependency, an env var that must be unset in a subprocess, etc. These are
the highest-value lines in the file. -->

## Existing-codebase edit discipline

When editing code that already exists:

- **Match the surrounding style.** Follow the conventions of the file and
  module you are in, not your own defaults.
- **Keep diffs minimal.** Change only what the task requires. A small,
  legible diff is easier to review and to revert.
- **No drive-by refactors.** Do not rename, reformat, reorder, or
  restructure code unrelated to the task. If you spot something worth
  fixing, note it separately rather than folding it into this change.

## Where things live

Pointers so an agent can orient without guessing:

- Plan / roadmap: `<path-to-plan>`
- Architecture notes: `<path-to-architecture-doc>`
- Contribution guide: `<path-to-contributing-doc>`
- Runner / task definitions: `<path-to-taskfile-or-makefile>`
- CI configuration: `<path-to-ci-config>`

